Well-definedness of b requires t ≠ 0 — t > 0 requires m < c: for x ≠ c. So the inverse is only defined for x ≠ c; at the center pick an arbitrary boundary point. In the surjectivity proof: by_cases h0 : x = center; then t = 0 case with b arbitrary.

Injectivity of the quotient lift map Φb:
Given Φb(p) = Φb(q): x = (1-t)c + t b = (1-t')c + t' b'.
Case t = 0: x = c pointwise. Then from (1-t')c + t' b' = c: if t' ≠ 0: b' = c pointwise. But b' ∈ simplexBoundarySet: 0 ∈ range b' — i.e. some coord 0; but c has all coords c > 0 — contradiction. So t' = 0 → right disjunct ✓.
Case t > 0: b = (x - (1-t)c)/t pointwise (b_i = (x_i - (1-t)c)/t). Claim t = t_max(x): 
- b_i ≥ 0 ∀i → (x_i - (1-t)c)/t ≥ 0 → x_i ≥ (1-t)c → x_i ≥ c(1-t)... For x_i < c: t ≥ (c - x_i)/c. Since t_max = max over i of (c - x_i)/c (only x_i < c contribute)... Actually: t ≥ (c-x_i)/c ∀i ⇔ t ≥ sup_i (c-x_i)/c = (c - m)/c = t_max. 
- 0 ∈ range b: some i with b_i = 0 → x_i = (1-t)c → t = (c - x_i)/c ≤ t_max. 
So t ≥ t_max and t ≤ t_max → t = t_max ✓ and b = b(x) ✓. Same for (b', t') → p = q ✓.

Note: the "t > 0" case needs x ≠ c implicitly (t > 0 → x ≠ c? if x = c then t(b - c) = 0 → b = c → contradiction as above; but we don't need that).

Also for the case t = 0 of injectivity we need: from Φb(q) = c pointwise and t' ≠ 0 get contradiction: b' = c pointwise but 0 ∈ range b': contradiction since all coords of c equal c > 0.

Alternatively a slicker argument: if t = 0, then x = c = (1-t')c + t'b'. Then t'(b' - c) = 0 pointwise. If t' = 0 → done. If t' ≠ 0 → b' = c pointwise; then 0 ∈ range b' → 0 = c contradiction ✓.

Surjectivity: for x: case x = c pointwise: t = 0, b arbitrary (exists: vertex b₀). Case x ≠ c pointwise: m < c: prove. Then t := (c-m)/c ∈ (0,1]: t ≤ 1: m ≥ 0 (x coords ≥ 0) → c - m ≤ c → t ≤ 1 ✓; t > 0: m < c ✓. b := b(x) ∈ boundary as above. Φb(b, t) = x pointwise: (1-t)c + t b_i = c - tc + tc + x_i - c = x_i ✓.

x ≠ c pointwise → m < c: m ≤ c via average: m ≤ (Σx)/(n+2): ciInf_le with the average? Hmm: ⨅ x_i ≤ average: standard: since ⨅ ≤ each x_i, (n+2)·⨅ ≤ Σ x_i = 1 → ⨅ ≤ 1/(n+2) = c. If m = c then: all x_i ≥ c = m and Σx_i = (n+2)c → each x_i = c (sum of nonneg = 0). So x ≠ c → m ≠ c → m < c ✓.

Formal detail: "x ≠ c pointwise": ¬ ((x : Fin (n+2) → ℝ) = fun _ => simplexCenter n). And "all coords ≥ c and sum forces equality" lemma: if ∀i, x_i ≥ c and Σx_i = (n+2)·c then x_i = c ∀i: sum_eq_zero: Σ (x_i - c) = 0 with each ≥ 0 → each = 0.

